WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.090
- So I am at Rockefeller right now.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:08.940
We are on the upper East side of New York.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.240
This is my boss's office.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.760
There's some historical
memorabilia behind me.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.123
We're gonna talk a little
bit about history here.

00:00:20.173 --> 00:00:24.290
And basically what we do here in this lab,

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:25.547
the lab is called

00:00:25.547 --> 00:00:29.360
"Laboratory of Chromatin
Biology and Epigenetics."

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.950
If you guys know what epigenetics is,

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:35.327
you can type in one in chat, if you want.

00:00:35.327 --> 00:00:37.503
If you don't, type a zero.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.773
I will tell you anyway,
what I think it is.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:43.136
So it doesn't really matter.

00:00:43.136 --> 00:00:45.130
(Alexey laughs)

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.090
And I guess there's more people joining in

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:50.010
and basically as...

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.040
Wow, there's a lot of people.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:53.920
Holy moly.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.080
I just gave a seminar on Zoom,
there was like 40 people.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.030
I thought this was like

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.253
the most people I've ever seen in a Zoom.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:02.630
There's still more people coming.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:04.303
Anyway,

00:01:05.929 --> 00:01:07.520
so big picture here,

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.560
and what we study and what
we're trying to understand

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.720
is something that has puzzled people

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.480
for a century probably now.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:16.360
Once they figured out

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.110
that genetic information is
stored in the chromosomes,

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.460
they also realized that
pretty much all cells

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:24.283
in the organism have the same chromosomes.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.483
But cells are different.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.050
So as the organism develops,

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.640
obviously, you start getting
neurons, and skin cells,

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.460
and bone cells, and blood cells,

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:40.430
and hundreds, thousands of
cell types in every organism.

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:41.620
But DNA is the same,

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:46.510
which is why you can catch
a criminal by DNA profile.

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:48.420
Or you can do ancestry by DNA profiling.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.260
And your DNA is not that different

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:51.820
from your mom's DNA or whatever.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:55.650
But the cells in your body
are completely different.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.450
They do completely different things.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:01.770
Sometimes these things go wrong.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.860
And sometimes that
causes genetic diseases,

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.950
which, sometimes it's
a mutation in the gene.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:07.200
And sometimes it's a mutation

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.193
that affects regulation of a gene.

00:02:12.005 --> 00:02:16.513
And broadly what has become
obvious in the past 50, 60 years

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:23.210
is that what matters is not
just the gene makeup you have

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:24.880
but also how these genes are regulated,

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.760
which ones of them are
on which ones are off.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.760
And how this on, off switch
goes between different states.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:35.733
Development, disease, and so on.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.140
So why does a skin cell
express skin genes?

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:43.883
Why does the brain cell grow
axons and become a brain cell?

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:49.540
Man, there's a lot of people.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:52.240
And they're still ding,
ding, ding, ding, ding.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:53.890
Okay.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:58.890
So what people have started
working on a while ago

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:05.560
is model organisms, because
we very rarely work on humans.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.680
Actually, now we do that quite a bit more,

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.100
because we have a lot of sequencing data

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.270
from human patients and
from different diseases,

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:14.490
and we can aggregate this big data thing,

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:19.200
But 50 years ago, that sequencing
wasn't really an option.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:20.570
So what they were working with

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:24.100
were fruit flies and ciliates.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.960
These little single cell organisms

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.830
that live in the little puddle of water.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:33.470
And something that my
boss became famous for

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:37.910
was that when he and
his predecessors looked

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:40.130
at these ciliate organisms,

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.510
they realized that they have two nuclei.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:44.740
You guys know what a nucleus is, right?

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:46.230
Okay, cool.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:47.592
So they have two nuclei.

00:03:47.592 --> 00:03:52.592
They have a micronucleus which
is very tiny and very dark,

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.730
if you look at it under a microscope.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:56.680
And they have a
macronucleus, which is huge,

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:58.677
and it has seemingly a lot more DNA.

00:03:58.677 --> 00:04:00.240
And the DNA is open,

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.713
and it looks like something's
going on in there.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.640
And so then they started
isolating proteins

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:06.710
from these nuclei,

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:09.750
and they realized that
the protein composition

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.803
is very different.

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:16.710
And while the core proteins are the same,

00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:19.040
they're modified in different way.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:20.830
So what am I talking about here?

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:21.663
Okay.

00:04:21.663 --> 00:04:26.650
So the blue stuff here, that's DNA.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:33.120
And it's basically a very
long string of A, T, G, and C.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:38.750
And the white stuff, it
looks like little pucks here,

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:40.720
that's the proteins we work on.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.661
And they're called histones.

00:04:42.661 --> 00:04:44.483
Who has heard of histones?

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:50.530
Yes, no? One in chat?

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:51.540
Anybody?

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.000
Yes. Two.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:54.030
Excellent.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:58.860
So histones are very small proteins

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:02.520
that are super conserved.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.470
Basically, you can find them...

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.150
The same histones in you

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:11.150
and in a potato and in a
cow and in a fruit fly.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.550
And for the most part, they
are pretty much identical.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:19.550
And because everything in
science is informed by evolution,

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:22.280
at least in biological science.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:25.610
If something is very
conserved between a jellyfish

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:30.050
and the human, it means
it's really important.

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:31.593
Like wow, important.

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:35.700
So when they started
looking at these proteins,

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:38.240
and it was back in the day,
like dark ages of science,

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.910
you couldn't just send
everything to a core facility,

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:43.890
get the sequence back and
get the results in the day.

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:45.660
You had to run everything by yourself.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:46.540
You couldn't buy kits.

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:48.740
You had to make your
reagents yourself, too.

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:51.930
They started sequencing
these proteins by hand.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:53.287
And they, basically,
started realizing that,

00:05:53.287 --> 00:05:55.620
"Oh my god, these proteins
are super conserved.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.390
They must be super boring," right?

00:05:58.390 --> 00:05:59.510
Wow. Super boring.

00:05:59.510 --> 00:06:01.020
Who cares about the protein

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:04.240
that is like a potato protein
and the human protein?

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:06.510
They're identical. Who
wants to study that?

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:08.600
Because everybody was studying DNA.

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:11.507
Watson and Crick just
published the DNA structure,

00:06:11.507 --> 00:06:13.730
and that was the thing.

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:16.270
Histones were thought
to be incredibly boring,

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:20.290
just packaging material that
DNA seems to wrap around.

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:21.840
Who cares?

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.380
Until they did the studies is
in these ciliate organisms,

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:30.733
little micronucleus, macronucleus.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.480
They're called Tetrahymena in Latin.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:39.220
And these ciliates happened
to have these two nuclei.

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:43.573
And in one nucleus, histones
carried one kind of mark.

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:45.570
And I'll explain what that means.

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:47.540
And then the other nucleus,

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:48.830
they carry the different kind of marks.

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:50.260
So what do I mean by that?

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:54.230
Well, it turns out that
in the micronucleus,

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:57.320
which is tiny, dark.

00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:59.060
It doesn't do anything.

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:04.060
Histones carried something
called methylation on them.

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:09.780
You guys know chemistry
probably better than I do.

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:12.253
You're like super, high
achieving students.

00:07:13.171 --> 00:07:15.910
So what they were able to deduce

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:20.910
is that a lot of histones
in that dark nucleus,

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:26.050
carry the post-translational
modification on them,

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:27.520
which means that the protein is the same

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:29.890
but there is a tiny little
chemical group sitting

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:31.653
on one of the amino acids.

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.023
And micronucleus was
very heavily methylated,

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:39.330
whereas in the macronucleus

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:42.293
which is transcriptionally
active and it does a lot stuff,

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:45.800
the histones carried another mark.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:47.673
And that was acetylation.

00:07:49.192 --> 00:07:52.259
And that's when things
started falling into place

00:07:52.259 --> 00:07:56.750
for the histones and
their role in biology.

00:07:56.750 --> 00:08:01.150
And behind me, I don't know
how much you guys can see that,

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:03.333
there's this super ancient whiteboard.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.850
And the sequences on this whiteboard

00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:09.940
are actually tails of four histones.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:11.300
There are four histone proteins.

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:13.490
And inside the nucleosome,
there is two of each.

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:14.770
So it's an octamer.

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:17.333
It's eight proteins
that all fold together.

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:20.690
And what people started noticing,

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:23.439
including the guy who I work for now,

00:08:23.439 --> 00:08:26.750
is that these lysines

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:29.550
or quite a few other amino acids there,

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:31.060
they carry these marks

00:08:33.064 --> 00:08:34.320
Acyl. Oh, camera.

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:35.763
Acyl, methyl.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:40.397
And depending on where these marks are

00:08:40.397 --> 00:08:44.823
and which marks they are, the
genes are either on or off.

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:49.110
And that became a huge breakthrough

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:51.950
in understanding both
the function of histones,

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:54.403
and also, generally, regulation of genes.

00:08:54.403 --> 00:08:58.350
Because now we're thinking
about not just DNA...

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:00.400
Oh, I didn't shut the door to the office.

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:07.250
Because now we're not just
talking about naked DNA,

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:09.990
and all of a sudden,
you have all these genes

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:11.530
and they seem to be doing the same stuff.

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:14.700
But how come some of them are
on and some of them are off?

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:16.980
Well, it turns out that
the genes that are off

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:21.030
are decorated by this
mark, broadly speaking.

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:22.503
And genes that are on

00:09:22.503 --> 00:09:25.583
are decorated by this
mark, broadly speaking.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:28.810
And, of course, that opened
up a bunch of other questions.

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:31.100
Like why? Who does this?

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:34.930
Who puts these marks on? Who
puts those other marks on?

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:37.220
How do these writing enzymes...

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:38.320
They call them writers.

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:40.790
How these reading enzymes,
how do they get there?

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:43.160
How do they know which genes
to decorate the right way

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:44.690
or whatever?

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:46.950
And to be honest, we don't
know all the answers.

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:50.404
I shared a couple of papers with you,

00:09:50.404 --> 00:09:53.630
and feel free to look through them,

00:09:53.630 --> 00:09:58.500
if you want more in-depth
understanding of this.

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:02.380
Again, in chat, who read anything?

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:04.700
Did anybody look through this?

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:05.900
It's okay if you didn't.

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:12.840
But this became the era of
what's called epigenetics

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:14.450
and we're still in that era.

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:16.300
It's still a very hot topic, I think.

00:10:17.590 --> 00:10:22.280
So epi means above, over, on top of.

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:24.950
And genetics means, you know, genetics.

00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:29.570
So epigenetics means regulation above DNA.

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:32.630
So what regulates the function of genes?

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:35.910
What regulates the gene
expression, basically.

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:37.550
Epigenetic processes.

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:39.420
And some people take this
term quite seriously.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:42.923
They actually think that
epigenetics means inheritance.

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:46.060
And maybe some of it is true.

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:48.790
We still don't have
the definitive evidence

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:50.367
of what's called epigenetic inheritance

00:10:50.367 --> 00:10:52.970
in higher organisms.

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:57.650
In organisms like worms, and
sometimes maybe fruit flies,

00:10:57.650 --> 00:10:59.300
maybe yeast,

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:02.330
changes to histone modifications

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:04.243
can propagate over many generations.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:10.570
So for example, a worm raised
on certain kinds of food,

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:12.830
if it gets used to that kind of food,

00:11:12.830 --> 00:11:15.710
it's offspring; it's
children of their children,

00:11:15.710 --> 00:11:18.290
their children of their children,
of two or three or four;

00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:21.650
they will have same modifications

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:23.133
over the same sets of genes.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:25.093
When people discovered it, it was like,

00:11:25.093 --> 00:11:26.300
"Oh my god, we figured it out.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:28.010
We figured out non-Mendelian inheritance."

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:29.810
That's like a holy grail of epigenetics,

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:32.613
to figure out non-Mendelian
inheritance patterns.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:37.070
It doesn't seem to hold true in humans.

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:38.824
Also, we don't really have
enough data in humans.

00:11:38.824 --> 00:11:40.664
And also you can't really dissect humans

00:11:40.664 --> 00:11:43.960
for your studies with every generation.

00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:45.510
It's a little more complicated.

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:51.620
But one thing we do study a lot,

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:55.070
and one thing that our lab
has studying a lot lately

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:57.623
is human diseases.

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:00.250
And specifically...

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:05.750
Something happened in the last 10 years

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:08.290
in biological sciences.

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:11.870
And I don't know, does
anybody want to take a guess?

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:16.730
So what was the biggest
breakthrough in the last 10 years

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:19.670
that basically changed our lives forever?

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:22.640
And it's not CRISPR. CRISPR is cool.

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:23.533
All right, Peter.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:26.790
Just go ahead and unmute yourselves.

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:28.743
- [Peter] The human genome was decoded.

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:31.463
- Human genome. Yes, absolutely.

00:12:32.314 --> 00:12:33.920
That's a great guess.

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:36.440
And I was kind of going that way.

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:39.000
I was just gonna say,
genome-wide sequencing.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:40.983
The next generation sequencing.

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:44.940
Does that mean you're raising your hand

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:46.140
or does it mean you are?

00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:48.503
I have...

00:12:48.503 --> 00:12:50.200
Odaelys, so I have your...

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:53.030
Oh, that's a clap, I
get it. Okay, I get it.

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:57.313
See, I'm terrible at Zoom.
I don't Zoom very well.

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:00.293
So, right. Next gen sequencing.

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:02.750
To be honest, the human genome,

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:04.890
they started the human genome with gels.

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:06.140
They were sequencing with gels.

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:07.560
And when they started it,

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:10.440
the people who were
criticizing that project

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:11.750
thought that the people were insane.

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:13.970
Like it's gonna take hundreds of years,

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:17.520
and millions and trillions
of hours, and dollars,

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:19.577
and thousands of people to do that.

00:13:19.577 --> 00:13:22.713
And now we can sequence human
genome in like the afternoon.

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:27.683
I can do it right here like it's nothing.

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:34.130
How far we went is absolutely amazing.

00:13:34.130 --> 00:13:36.060
And that's basically what happened.

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:40.940
Now it's a no-brainer, if you
have a tumor in a patient,

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:42.640
to take the tumor and sequence it.

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:45.520
It's a no-brainer if you have a patient

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:48.820
with developmental disorder
to sequence that patient.

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:52.853
And as our numbers of
sequenced patients grow,

00:13:54.090 --> 00:13:56.380
what happens is we can
start seeing the patterns.

00:13:56.380 --> 00:13:59.170
Because you only have five patients,

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:01.000
if you only have five tumors,

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:03.390
you can't really see
very much of anything.

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:05.100
That's why NFL games are so unpredictable,

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:07.190
because there's only 16
of them in the season.

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:08.430
And it's a small number,

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:11.750
you can't really make any
long-term predictions over it.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:13.950
If you're sequencing
thousands and thousands

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:16.970
and thousands of patients,
essentially every month,

00:14:16.970 --> 00:14:20.950
over the course of five years,

00:14:20.950 --> 00:14:22.700
you're gonna accumulate enough data

00:14:22.700 --> 00:14:24.023
to start seeing patterns.

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:27.720
And what I'm talking about here...

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:29.340
And it's very relevant to cancer,

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:31.980
but it also applies to
developmental disorders,

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:33.846
schizophrenia, autism, you name it.

00:14:33.846 --> 00:14:34.679
Anything.

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:37.980
What I'm gonna show you here kind of

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:42.230
like a little explanation
of what this means.

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:46.873
So we take the tumor and
we take normal cells.

00:14:47.780 --> 00:14:51.570
Normal cells are usually
just peripheral blood cells,

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:54.890
or you can take them from
the tumor biopsy as well.

00:14:54.890 --> 00:14:56.463
And then you sequence both.

00:14:57.670 --> 00:14:59.440
And if you noticed,

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:02.760
I basically compiled a list of mutations.

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:05.680
So on this side, there's these lines

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:08.500
and the vertical lines basically
mean there's a mutation

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:09.433
in that position.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:14.920
And no genome is completely
free of differences

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:16.130
from reference.

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:17.420
Every one of us is different.

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:21.420
By every thousand basis,
there is a mutation.

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:24.940
So it's really important to do this,

00:15:24.940 --> 00:15:26.423
control the normal cells.

00:15:28.710 --> 00:15:32.440
But then if you compare normal
cells from the same patient

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:34.730
to the tumor cells in that same patient,

00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:38.880
very likely, you're gonna
start seeing differences.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:41.460
And these differences are
usually numbering and hundreds,

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:42.503
if not thousands.

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:46.440
But if you do more than five patients,

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:48.890
you can start seeing really
interesting patterns.

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:52.460
And these patterns can be like this.

00:15:52.460 --> 00:15:57.243
For example, you can have
a protein in a tumor.

00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:02.313
So I drew out here a
structure of a protein.

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:06.050
And all of a sudden, in this position,

00:16:06.050 --> 00:16:09.550
we have lots of patients
who have mutations.

00:16:09.550 --> 00:16:11.200
So what does that tell you?

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:12.493
Somebody, anybody.

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:15.773
Unmute yourself.

00:16:19.065 --> 00:16:23.290
- [Student] That if there's a
lot of mutations in that area,

00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:26.473
then that might be the
direct cause of the tumor.

00:16:27.654 --> 00:16:28.487
- Yeah, that's a good guess.

00:16:28.487 --> 00:16:30.440
It tells you that that's
probably important, right?

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:32.570
That's something to look at.

00:16:32.570 --> 00:16:34.200
You don't know the direct cause yet,

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:35.750
but that's a very good guess.

00:16:35.750 --> 00:16:38.430
And if you see something
like that, that's wow.

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:39.870
That's really cool. That's promising.

00:16:39.870 --> 00:16:41.750
You want to study that.

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:43.730
You can also start seeing that...

00:16:43.730 --> 00:16:47.570
Let's say, every patient
with that particular tumor

00:16:48.790 --> 00:16:50.930
has a mutation in the protein.

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:55.833
But mutations are randomly
distributed in that one protein.

00:16:56.751 --> 00:16:58.573
And that also tells you something.

00:16:59.570 --> 00:17:02.140
Like this, in the first case scenario,

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:04.380
this tells you that this particular domain

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:08.090
or this particular area of the
protein is really important.

00:17:08.090 --> 00:17:10.810
There are proteins like that in cancer.

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:12.610
This is the second case scenario,

00:17:12.610 --> 00:17:14.790
which tells you that
it's maybe not a domain

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:16.280
of a protein that's important,

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:19.420
but the protein itself is
really, really important.

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:22.390
And any mutation in this protein

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:25.220
can be very detrimental to the patient.

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:26.290
And probably not quite...

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:27.440
It's actually the opposite.

00:17:27.440 --> 00:17:28.930
Very beneficial to the tumor

00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:30.690
because it makes the tumor grow.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:33.630
If you guys could have heard
about a protein called p53.

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:35.170
You probably heard about p53.

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:37.023
So p53 looks like this.

00:17:38.740 --> 00:17:41.520
It's mutated in half of all cancers

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:44.010
and mutations are all over the place.

00:17:44.010 --> 00:17:46.203
If you heard about the protein called Ras.

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:51.602
It's also mutated in, I don't
know, 30, 40% of all cancers.

00:17:51.602 --> 00:17:54.200
It looks like this. In fact,
it doesn't even look like that.

00:17:54.200 --> 00:17:56.530
It looks just like one mutation.

00:17:56.530 --> 00:18:00.560
That one mutation is just so detrimental,

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:02.820
so terrible that you can get cancer.

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:05.640
You can get another kind of mutation,

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:06.890
and that's the other way around,

00:18:06.890 --> 00:18:11.890
you can have basically
blank areas in the proteins.

00:18:12.290 --> 00:18:13.500
And that tells you that that part

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:17.340
of the protein is just
important for viability.

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:21.400
You can not have that part mutated.

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:22.390
Going back to the histones.

00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:26.720
Why are histones conserved
between potatoes and humans?

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:30.940
Well, it's because they're so
important that any mutation

00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:33.880
in these proteins is
very poorly tolerated.

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:37.750
And you don't get any benefits
from having that mutation.

00:18:37.750 --> 00:18:41.260
So evolution is always
in our back of our minds.

00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:44.110
We always think about
what is the pressures,

00:18:44.110 --> 00:18:45.950
what is the benefits of this mutation,

00:18:45.950 --> 00:18:47.533
why does this mutation exists.

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:51.093
All right.

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:57.000
Tell me something you guys want
to know in the next minute.

00:18:58.360 --> 00:19:00.780
I'm gonna take a little break talking,

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:03.110
and maybe you guys can tell me,

00:19:03.110 --> 00:19:04.943
what do you know about epigenetics,

00:19:04.943 --> 00:19:06.410
what do you know about histones,

00:19:06.410 --> 00:19:08.660
what do you know about cancer biology.

00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:10.610
And if I can answer any of your questions.

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:11.443
And after that,

00:19:11.443 --> 00:19:14.680
I will go back, talking
about histone mutations.

00:19:14.680 --> 00:19:16.650
And then we're gonna take a
