Okay. All right.

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:32.363
So each one of you, go
ahead and unmute yourselves,

00:19:35.459 --> 00:19:37.010
and maybe ask me questions.

00:19:37.010 --> 00:19:40.223
Maybe I can tell you
how I came to this lab.

00:19:43.630 --> 00:19:44.570
Yeah, there we go.

00:19:44.570 --> 00:19:46.420
There's like 40 of you guys. Come on.

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:51.590
You probably have questions.

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:55.140
- Well, so I was reading
the just the overview

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:03.400
of what you guys do in this lab on the...

00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:08.580
And it says at the beginning...

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:13.580
Like it mentioned some enzymatic
processes at the beginning.

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:18.880
And I would just like
to know, what are these?

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:23.880
Like acyletation, methylation,
phosphorylation, and...

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:26.110
- And everything.

00:20:26.110 --> 00:20:30.430
Yeah. So how deep do you
want me to go on this?

00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:33.210
All right, so we can go pretty deep.

00:20:33.210 --> 00:20:35.370
So when we talk about histones,

00:20:35.370 --> 00:20:37.500
and when we talk about
all enzymatic processes

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:39.910
and the whole concept of these

00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:45.560
in biology that we study is...

00:20:45.560 --> 00:20:49.460
there's this pretty artificial
division between readers,

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:53.310
writers, and erasers, right?

00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:57.780
And it's artificial, because
the same enzyme can be a reader

00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:01.609
and an eraser and maybe a writer too.

00:21:01.609 --> 00:21:03.230
And if you're writing a mark above a mark,

00:21:03.230 --> 00:21:06.150
then you are also an eraser
and reader and writer

00:21:06.150 --> 00:21:06.983
at the same time.

00:21:06.983 --> 00:21:08.760
'Cause you have to figure
all these marks out

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:09.610
at the same time.

00:21:10.450 --> 00:21:12.870
So what happens with
histones and what happens

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:16.770
with our genome that's
packaged by histones,

00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:21.770
is that histones are decorated
by dozens, if not hundreds

00:21:22.870 --> 00:21:25.503
of different enzymatic
modifications on them.

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:29.600
And the enzymes that do that obviously

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:32.770
have to come to the right
place at the right time,

00:21:32.770 --> 00:21:37.210
put this mark, and then
either leave or move on

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:39.590
to the next unit.

00:21:39.590 --> 00:21:41.860
'Cause this is really
what chromatin looks like.

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:43.300
People who have studied this

00:21:43.300 --> 00:21:46.140
using electron microscopes many years ago,

00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:48.840
they've realized that it
looks like beads on a string,

00:21:49.890 --> 00:21:52.760
and the beads are nucleosomes
and the string is DNA.

00:21:52.760 --> 00:21:56.060
And basically, DNA is
wrapped around nucleosomes,

00:21:56.060 --> 00:21:58.913
and it's interpreted by the cell,

00:22:00.110 --> 00:22:01.733
in context of these nucleosomes.

00:22:03.434 --> 00:22:06.340
So these enzymes that work on DNA,

00:22:06.340 --> 00:22:09.770
we don't have a lot of high
quality imaging studies on them.

00:22:09.770 --> 00:22:11.903
So if you guys wanna ever
go ahead and do that,

00:22:11.903 --> 00:22:13.100
that'd be super cool.

00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:14.800
I'm sure technology will catch up.

00:22:16.620 --> 00:22:20.020
They have to go from one
nucleosome to another,

00:22:20.020 --> 00:22:22.270
and they have to modify
them in certain ways.

00:22:24.910 --> 00:22:27.503
Let me just share a screen
with you real quick.

00:22:30.660 --> 00:22:31.573
Share screen.

00:22:33.110 --> 00:22:34.833
So this is the structure.

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:41.780
Can you see my screen with a
