00:22:41.890 --> 00:22:43.010
All right. Excellent.

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:44.890
So this is a structure I shared with you

00:22:44.890 --> 00:22:46.230
in that email as well.

00:22:46.230 --> 00:22:48.990
And this is basically a nucleosome.

00:22:51.350 --> 00:22:53.930
All right. So that's what it looks like.

00:22:53.930 --> 00:22:57.080
If you look at the DNA

00:22:57.080 --> 00:23:01.063
and if you look at how the
proteins are folded inside it.

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:05.143
Oh, sorry. Hold on.

00:23:05.143 --> 00:23:07.053
I have a random incoming spam phone call.

00:23:09.940 --> 00:23:14.000
So there are four histone types.

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:15.450
They're all folded together

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:19.180
into this beautiful ribbon structure.

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:20.870
And then there's a fifth histone type

00:23:20.870 --> 00:23:22.020
which is called linker histone,

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:24.510
which sits just outside the nucleosome.

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:29.020
And basically every one
of them looks like this.

00:23:29.020 --> 00:23:31.420
Now, histones also have tails.

00:23:31.420 --> 00:23:34.150
And the tails are what's drawn
on that structure over there,

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:36.210
on that board over there.

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:39.810
But they can be modified inside
the core of the nucleosome

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:41.590
or it can be modified on the tails.

00:23:41.590 --> 00:23:43.630
And these tails also,
they look like antennas

00:23:43.630 --> 00:23:46.440
that sense stuff.

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:48.090
You can't see the tails in the structure,

00:23:48.090 --> 00:23:50.010
simply because they are floppy.

00:23:50.010 --> 00:23:52.740
They don't have a structure,
they're just everywhere.

00:23:52.740 --> 00:23:54.140
They sample the environment.

00:23:55.440 --> 00:23:57.500
To give you an example

00:23:57.500 --> 00:24:02.030
of what an enzyme will look like

00:24:02.030 --> 00:24:05.970
when it's reading or
writing these modifications,

00:24:05.970 --> 00:24:07.910
here's another structure.

00:24:07.910 --> 00:24:11.270
And this is a much
worse quality structure.

00:24:11.270 --> 00:24:15.533
But it made big waves in
science when it was published.

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:20.523
This is a very important
complex called Polycomb.

00:24:22.280 --> 00:24:24.700
I am not sure if you have heard of it.

00:24:24.700 --> 00:24:27.900
But if you do study anything epigenetic,

00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:30.063
you probably have heard about it.

00:24:31.570 --> 00:24:33.560
So this is the nucleosome.

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:35.460
And this is another nucleosome.

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:38.390
And what this Polycomb complex does

00:24:38.390 --> 00:24:41.647
is it senses a modification
on one nucleosome-

00:24:41.647 --> 00:24:44.180
- [Student] It's still showing
the previous structure.

00:24:44.180 --> 00:24:46.030
- Oh, sorry, sorry, sorry. Hold on.

00:24:46.030 --> 00:24:47.063
New share.

00:24:48.989 --> 00:24:50.589
Ah, there we go. How about that?

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:53.273
Excellent. All right.

00:24:53.273 --> 00:24:54.106
So here we go.

00:24:54.106 --> 00:24:55.440
So we have two nucleosomes now.

00:24:56.330 --> 00:24:59.020
I know why. it's because I
opened it in a different window.

00:24:59.020 --> 00:25:01.140
And I'm still sharing the
same window. Okay, cool.

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:06.040
So this is the same ribbon diagram,

00:25:06.040 --> 00:25:11.033
and it senses one nucleosome
here and then it reaches over.

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:14.800
And if you zoom all the way
in, you see this blue line?

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:16.540
That's the histone.

00:25:16.540 --> 00:25:17.530
And then you don't really see it.

00:25:17.530 --> 00:25:19.490
And then you see it again over here.

00:25:19.490 --> 00:25:21.470
And that's a histone tail.

00:25:21.470 --> 00:25:23.940
And this histone tail
is gonna get modified.

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:26.710
A methyl mark is gonna get put on it

00:25:26.710 --> 00:25:30.373
by this enzyme that's shown
here in pink or magenta.

00:25:31.220 --> 00:25:34.070
And this complex has at least
four different sub units.

00:25:34.070 --> 00:25:35.140
They're very complicated.

00:25:35.140 --> 00:25:37.320
There is probably five
more that come around

00:25:37.320 --> 00:25:38.320
and do other things.

00:25:39.390 --> 00:25:42.260
This complex is very heavily
mutated in some cancers,

00:25:42.260 --> 00:25:45.330
including lymphomas.

00:25:45.330 --> 00:25:49.203
And the mutations, just like
in this picture I showed you,

00:25:51.010 --> 00:25:53.900
the mutations in lymphomas
all happen right here

00:25:53.900 --> 00:25:55.303
in this catalytic center.

00:25:56.920 --> 00:25:58.230
And this enzyme just goes crazy

00:25:58.230 --> 00:26:01.610
and it starts putting mark
over and over and over again.

00:26:01.610 --> 00:26:02.840
Normally, it's regulated.

00:26:02.840 --> 00:26:06.473
And in lymphomas, it just goes nuts.

00:26:07.760 --> 00:26:10.360
Now, Polycomb is very special,
and it's very historic,

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:11.490
and very interesting.

00:26:11.490 --> 00:26:15.260
Because when it was discovered,

00:26:15.260 --> 00:26:17.910
people actually didn't know about histones

00:26:17.910 --> 00:26:20.390
and histone modifications.

00:26:20.390 --> 00:26:23.383
It was discovered by a guy
and, actually, his wife.

00:26:24.295 --> 00:26:25.128
I'm forgetting her name.

00:26:25.128 --> 00:26:28.153
His last name was Louis. Her
last name was Louis, too.

00:26:30.190 --> 00:26:32.840
They were screening fruit flies

00:26:32.840 --> 00:26:37.160
for mutations that affected
their body patterning.

00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:38.870
'Cause they were interested in regulation

00:26:38.870 --> 00:26:42.700
of how our body pattern's specified.

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:46.190
How does fly know to grow six legs?

00:26:46.190 --> 00:26:51.110
And how does it grow two
legs on each body segment

00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:55.860
and not an extra pair of
wings and all that stuff.

00:26:55.860 --> 00:26:57.610
And so he was looking for mutations

00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:00.920
that change that pattern.

00:27:00.920 --> 00:27:05.090
And he basically had flies
that have extra pair of wings,

00:27:05.090 --> 00:27:07.740
extra pair of leg segments,

00:27:07.740 --> 00:27:09.783
extra pair of abdominal segments.

00:27:10.780 --> 00:27:11.980
He ended up winning a Nobel prize

00:27:11.980 --> 00:27:14.080
for those studies, by the way.

00:27:14.080 --> 00:27:17.950
And just by genetics, just by
crossing these flies together,

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:22.460
he figured out that there is a complex

00:27:22.460 --> 00:27:27.460
of proteins that does something
to repress a set of genes.

00:27:27.850 --> 00:27:29.350
He didn't know how it does it.

00:27:30.310 --> 00:27:32.823
And I'll tell you another cool
story about it in a second.

00:27:34.948 --> 00:27:36.670
And he called it Polycomb,

00:27:36.670 --> 00:27:38.720
because the phenotype of these flies,

00:27:38.720 --> 00:27:42.510
they had extra bristle
combs on their legs.

00:27:42.510 --> 00:27:45.080
Who would notice an
extra bunch of bristles

00:27:45.080 --> 00:27:46.853
on the legs of a fruit fly, right?

00:27:47.940 --> 00:27:52.043
But the name stuck and we're
all stuck with that name.

00:27:53.381 --> 00:27:56.370
And Polycomb is probably the
hottest enzymatic complex

00:27:56.370 --> 00:27:59.020
in histone biology now.

00:27:59.020 --> 00:28:01.270
But here's a super cool story.

00:28:01.270 --> 00:28:06.270
So there is a repressor
complex in flies and humans,

00:28:07.000 --> 00:28:11.413
that, in flies was identified
on a chromosome three,

00:28:14.330 --> 00:28:16.973
I think on the band
nine of the chromosome.

00:28:18.130 --> 00:28:20.313
And it's called SU(VAR)3-9.

00:28:21.220 --> 00:28:23.940
SU(VAR) means suppressor of variegation.

00:28:23.940 --> 00:28:25.130
It's a very genetic term.

00:28:25.130 --> 00:28:26.710
It's just the screen type

00:28:26.710 --> 00:28:28.883
in which this protein was identified, 3-9.

00:28:30.220 --> 00:28:31.770
And here's the super cool part:

00:28:33.900 --> 00:28:37.940
30 years later, they figured
out how that complex works.

00:28:37.940 --> 00:28:40.010
Do you know what it does?

00:28:40.010 --> 00:28:44.863
It modifies lysine on histone
three in position nine.

00:28:46.470 --> 00:28:49.135
It's just a coincidence,
but it's super cool.

00:28:49.135 --> 00:28:52.730
It's like hand of God-type
situation. SU(VAR)3-9.

00:28:52.730 --> 00:28:54.257
And everybody who you talk
to you now, they're like,

00:28:54.257 --> 00:28:58.450
"Oh, yeah. SU(VAR)3-9
because it's the histone H3

00:28:58.450 --> 00:29:01.410
and lysine 9 methyltransferase."

00:29:01.410 --> 00:29:02.243
No. No, no, no.

00:29:02.243 --> 00:29:05.150
They named it 30 years before
they knew what it does.

00:29:05.150 --> 00:29:08.603
And it's just an accident
that the name matches so well.

00:29:10.180 --> 00:29:13.620
Anyway, so this is

00:29:13.620 --> 00:29:15.750
what the enzymatic complex will look like.

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:18.843
And the structure was made
just about a year ago.

00:29:20.450 --> 00:29:22.863
We finally have the tools
to do this kind of stuff.

