00:30:19.210 --> 00:30:23.570
So there's two things in that question.

00:30:23.570 --> 00:30:27.323
First is how does the protein
fold correctly in the cell?

00:30:28.410 --> 00:30:31.253
And to be honest, we don't really know.

00:30:32.690 --> 00:30:34.609
There is a lot of talk about chaperones.

00:30:34.609 --> 00:30:36.640
And obviously chaperones
are super important.

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:38.960
There's a lot of proteins that come in

00:30:38.960 --> 00:30:40.640
and make sure protein stays

00:30:40.640 --> 00:30:43.290
in certain conformation during folding.

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:45.220
It becomes quite complex

00:30:45.220 --> 00:30:46.830
when we're talking about
multi-protein structure.

00:30:46.830 --> 00:30:49.430
For example, the nucleosome.

00:30:49.430 --> 00:30:52.633
Because we have, essentially,
eight different proteins

00:30:52.633 --> 00:30:54.180
that have to get together

00:30:54.180 --> 00:30:56.150
in the right time, the right place.

00:30:56.150 --> 00:30:57.350
What's curious about nucleosome

00:30:57.350 --> 00:31:00.870
is that you can actually
purify histones from bacteria,

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:03.320
which we do routinely
in the lab every day.

00:31:03.320 --> 00:31:05.560
And if you add them in the right order

00:31:05.560 --> 00:31:08.030
with the fragment of DNA,

00:31:08.030 --> 00:31:12.556
they will just fold
themselves into a nucleosome.

00:31:12.556 --> 00:31:15.150
They know. How they know, I don't know.

00:31:15.150 --> 00:31:19.530
But they find their partners,
and just energetically,

00:31:19.530 --> 00:31:20.363
it seems like

00:31:20.363 --> 00:31:23.730
there's this one absolutely
optimal confirmation

00:31:23.730 --> 00:31:26.630
that you can then figure out.

