00:32:21.690 --> 00:32:23.460
So one question is, how do proteins know

00:32:23.460 --> 00:32:25.710
how to solve this structure, right?

00:32:25.710 --> 00:32:27.950
And somehow they do. We
don't know the answer.

00:32:27.950 --> 00:32:29.680
Another question is,

00:32:29.680 --> 00:32:32.520
how do scientists know
what this looks like?

00:32:32.520 --> 00:32:34.520
And that I can tell you more about.

00:32:34.520 --> 00:32:38.100
And the answer is, until very recently,

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:40.943
we did something called
x-ray crystallography.

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:45.520
And to do that, you have
to have very pure protein

00:32:46.750 --> 00:32:49.290
and you have to figure out a condition

00:32:49.290 --> 00:32:53.780
in which this very pure
protein will assemble

00:32:53.780 --> 00:32:58.240
into, essentially,
what's a crystal, right?

00:32:58.240 --> 00:33:03.240
A crystal is a packed unit where each cell

00:33:04.910 --> 00:33:09.123
is attached to adjacent
cells in a stereotypical way.

00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:14.410
So if you play with salt,
play with detergents,

00:33:14.410 --> 00:33:19.410
play with solution amounts
and concentrations,

00:33:19.780 --> 00:33:21.330
and usually people who do that,

00:33:21.330 --> 00:33:23.160
they do it on a very large scale way.

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:25.860
They have robots doing that.

00:33:25.860 --> 00:33:27.180
These robots just look like boxes.

00:33:27.180 --> 00:33:30.330
So they're not like cool
robots from Futurama.

00:33:30.330 --> 00:33:33.000
They're actually very boring robots.

00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:35.410
And you have to put a
bunch of solutions in them,

00:33:35.410 --> 00:33:38.830
and they pipettes tiny, tiny
droplets all these solutions,

00:33:38.830 --> 00:33:40.060
and then you wait.

00:33:40.060 --> 00:33:42.250
And then a month later,

00:33:42.250 --> 00:33:45.150
you screen through these plates
and you look for crystals.

00:33:46.130 --> 00:33:48.910
And if you're lucky, you
will have crystals growing.

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:51.790
And that means that
you're close to conditions

00:33:51.790 --> 00:33:53.813
in which the proteins can crystallize.

00:33:54.830 --> 00:33:57.440
This was also discovered a long time ago.

00:33:57.440 --> 00:33:59.130
People who first discovered it, did it,

00:33:59.130 --> 00:34:00.473
I think for hemoglobin.

00:34:01.930 --> 00:34:04.470
And they also won a Nobel prize for it.

00:34:04.470 --> 00:34:08.780
But what they did was that
they put a beam of x-ray,

00:34:08.780 --> 00:34:12.520
and they do x-ray because x-ray
has much shorter wavelength.

00:34:12.520 --> 00:34:15.500
And so if you put like regular light over,

00:34:15.500 --> 00:34:18.700
obviously the distances between the atoms

00:34:18.700 --> 00:34:21.710
in this structure are on
the order of angstroms.

00:34:21.710 --> 00:34:25.153
So it's 1/10th of a nanometer.
It's very, very, very close.

00:34:26.260 --> 00:34:31.260
You can sort of zoom in and I
can randomly color a sample.

00:34:33.100 --> 00:34:38.100
So let's do a residue and we
can highlight the residue.

00:34:40.200 --> 00:34:42.770
Hold on, I have Zoom interface over me.

00:34:42.770 --> 00:34:44.543
Color, reds.

00:34:45.530 --> 00:34:49.950
And we will show sticks.

00:34:49.950 --> 00:34:51.270
So there we go.

00:34:51.270 --> 00:34:52.823
So this looks like a tyrosine.

00:34:54.100 --> 00:34:55.353
This is an amino acid.

00:34:57.010 --> 00:34:59.350
Is that a tyrosine? I'm
gonna double check myself.

00:34:59.350 --> 00:35:01.010
Yes, it is.

00:35:01.010 --> 00:35:04.800
So this is a tyrosine.
And it has this ring.

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:07.750
And this ring has atomic density that

00:35:07.750 --> 00:35:10.670
by shining x-ray light at the crystal,

00:35:10.670 --> 00:35:12.110
you can collect the scatter pattern

00:35:12.110 --> 00:35:13.360
and you can resolve that.

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:18.300
At first, they did it by
hand and it took years

00:35:18.300 --> 00:35:23.030
to just match the dots to
possible distances between atoms.

00:35:23.030 --> 00:35:24.370
And you couldn't really do it

00:35:24.370 --> 00:35:27.433
with complex structures like this.

00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:31.210
As time goes on, obviously,

00:35:31.210 --> 00:35:36.210
we go from regular radio
to an iPhone 12G, whatever.

00:35:36.850 --> 00:35:40.810
And now we can plug it into a computer,

00:35:40.810 --> 00:35:43.040
and the computer does
the math much better than

00:35:43.040 --> 00:35:44.810
a regular person can.

00:35:44.810 --> 00:35:46.720
So that was x-ray.

00:35:46.720 --> 00:35:48.910
The problem with x-ray crystallography

00:35:48.910 --> 00:35:50.940
is actually a huge problem.

00:35:50.940 --> 00:35:54.583
You have to have a
crystal and that's hard.

00:35:54.583 --> 00:35:57.610
The biggest problem is not
the resolve the crystal.

00:35:57.610 --> 00:35:59.940
You can resolve anything.

00:35:59.940 --> 00:36:01.560
The problem is to grow it.

00:36:01.560 --> 00:36:02.970
And the bigger your protein is

00:36:02.970 --> 00:36:05.610
and the harder it is
to purify your protein,

00:36:05.610 --> 00:36:08.520
the less chances are you're
gonna grow a crystal ever.

00:36:08.520 --> 00:36:11.230
And people spend years and
years and years pursuing that

00:36:11.230 --> 00:36:13.883
for some proteins and they
could never grow a crystal.

00:36:14.970 --> 00:36:15.950
That's how science works, right?

00:36:15.950 --> 00:36:19.713
You spend years doing something
and then it doesn't work.

00:36:22.290 --> 00:36:25.870
It's actually quite sad and funny

00:36:25.870 --> 00:36:27.973
when it's not happening to you, I guess.

00:36:29.060 --> 00:36:32.300
But then a few years
ago, something happened,

00:36:32.300 --> 00:36:35.690
and what happened again, was
this big data revolution.

00:36:35.690 --> 00:36:38.593
And the big data came in
terms of image analysis.

00:36:39.730 --> 00:36:44.130
And once people had the
capacity to analyze images

00:36:44.130 --> 00:36:49.130
at an insane scale of millions
and millions of millions

00:36:50.910 --> 00:36:52.670
of photographs at the same time.

00:36:52.670 --> 00:36:54.090
It's the kind of the same software

00:36:54.090 --> 00:36:56.010
that can do the facial recognition

00:36:56.010 --> 00:36:59.340
and license plates
identification on the cars.

00:36:59.340 --> 00:37:01.170
Happened at the same time.

00:37:01.170 --> 00:37:02.930
They also realized that
they can take pictures

00:37:02.930 --> 00:37:07.060
with electron microscope of the same beads

00:37:07.060 --> 00:37:08.960
on the string I was telling you about.

00:37:09.860 --> 00:37:12.950
So what you do is you take your proteins,

00:37:12.950 --> 00:37:15.190
and it can be a very
large protein complex now.

00:37:15.190 --> 00:37:17.460
It can be a protein complex
that's very hard to purify.

00:37:17.460 --> 00:37:19.040
It doesn't matter.

00:37:19.040 --> 00:37:21.803
You put a very thin layer on the slide,

00:37:22.950 --> 00:37:26.113
and you plug it into the
cryo-electron microscope.

00:37:27.830 --> 00:37:31.300
So at very, very cold temperatures,

00:37:31.300 --> 00:37:34.390
the electron light, same
electron light shines

00:37:34.390 --> 00:37:35.540
on these protein complexes

00:37:35.540 --> 00:37:38.530
and you get the actual photograph.

00:37:38.530 --> 00:37:40.840
And then you take these photographs
and you take millions...

00:37:40.840 --> 00:37:43.760
Well, not millions, but tens of thousands,

00:37:43.760 --> 00:37:46.540
hundreds of thousands of them,
you plug them into software.

00:37:46.540 --> 00:37:48.490
Software starts figuring out,

00:37:48.490 --> 00:37:50.340
are there any stereotypical patterns?

00:37:51.190 --> 00:37:53.120
And the more photographs you take,

00:37:53.120 --> 00:37:55.363
the more precise can software be.

00:37:56.220 --> 00:37:58.790
And, actually, the
structure I was showing you

00:37:58.790 --> 00:38:01.440
for the Polycomb complex,
that's how it was obtained.

00:38:02.670 --> 00:38:06.750
So now, the electron
densities can be derived

00:38:06.750 --> 00:38:08.143
from just the photograph.

00:38:09.800 --> 00:38:12.040
And that has revolutionized
crystallography.

00:38:12.040 --> 00:38:16.260
Everybody who is doing x-ray
crystals is now out of a job.

00:38:16.260 --> 00:38:20.640
Not really, but they have
to change their ways.

00:38:20.640 --> 00:38:21.710
And people who know how

00:38:21.710 --> 00:38:23.870
to operate these cryo-electron microscopes

00:38:23.870 --> 00:38:28.460
are absolutely top scientists and so on.

00:38:28.460 --> 00:38:31.730
They won Nobel prizes for
that technology as well.

00:38:31.730 --> 00:38:35.180
And now with Google AI on the way,

00:38:35.180 --> 00:38:36.960
I think five years down the road,

00:38:36.960 --> 00:38:39.090
they will be looking for jobs too.

00:38:39.090 --> 00:38:42.360
So that's progress. That's how it works.

00:38:42.360 --> 00:38:43.660
And that's quite exciting.

00:38:44.970 --> 00:38:48.053
So that's how these
structures are derived.

00:38:50.682 --> 00:38:53.700
What I personally do is I
