I don't think you can ever see the color.

00:43:17.930 --> 00:43:19.958
So what are you gonna do with these cells?

00:43:19.958 --> 00:43:23.000
(Alexey's colleague responds)

00:43:23.000 --> 00:43:23.833
Oh, there we go.

00:43:26.520 --> 00:43:27.353
There we go.

00:43:27.353 --> 00:43:29.263
So that's exactly that.
We're gonna do Western blot.

00:43:30.580 --> 00:43:32.330
We do a lot of Westerns in the lab.

00:43:33.270 --> 00:43:36.293
These are shaker incubators,
where we grow bacteria.

00:43:37.680 --> 00:43:40.130
You can grow with them at
different temperatures.

00:43:41.470 --> 00:43:46.010
These are tanks where we store...

00:43:54.370 --> 00:43:56.150
It's the masks. Yes.

00:43:56.150 --> 00:44:00.863
So this is the temperature.

00:44:04.550 --> 00:44:06.006
And this is super duper cool.

00:44:06.006 --> 00:44:07.656
This is where we store the cells.

00:44:09.160 --> 00:44:10.483
And it's liquid nitrogen.

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:15.500
Boom.

00:44:15.500 --> 00:44:17.463
On -142.

00:44:22.300 --> 00:44:25.910
This is the tissue culture.

00:44:25.910 --> 00:44:29.100
It might get a little spotty
with the coverage here

00:44:30.580 --> 00:44:33.823
because I think this is
where our network fails.

00:44:35.819 --> 00:44:37.960
And this is what the cells look like

00:44:37.960 --> 00:44:39.210
when we grow them in culture.

00:44:39.210 --> 00:44:42.933
You can't actually see the
cells, but that's the plates,

00:44:45.290 --> 00:44:48.510
and that's the (indiscernible)

00:44:51.429 --> 00:44:54.150
And then this is where they live.

00:44:54.150 --> 00:44:58.383
You can grow regular cells,
you can grow cancer cells.

00:45:06.530 --> 00:45:11.160
And it looks really
dry, like a little rusty

00:45:11.160 --> 00:45:12.740
but that's actually copper.

00:45:12.740 --> 00:45:15.883
And copper is killing bacteria and fungi.

00:45:16.840 --> 00:45:20.310
So the copper lining of the incubators

00:45:20.310 --> 00:45:21.460
is actually quite good.

00:45:23.310 --> 00:45:24.700
And I'm gonna show you
a couple more things

00:45:24.700 --> 00:45:25.533
in another room.

00:45:25.533 --> 00:45:27.660
And chances are, you guys
are gonna break off here

00:45:27.660 --> 00:45:30.523
because we don't have any
coverage here on the network.

00:45:32.490 --> 00:45:34.960
But here is the part of the lab

00:45:34.960 --> 00:45:37.883
where we sometimes work
with radioactivity.

00:45:39.040 --> 00:45:43.210
Like if you ever want to
trace a special protein

00:45:43.210 --> 00:45:44.940
that you're interested in,

00:45:44.940 --> 00:45:46.800
sometimes the easiest way to do so

00:45:46.800 --> 00:45:50.603
is to put in radioactive
amino acid into it.

00:45:52.087 --> 00:45:55.200
And then you can track it.
You'll always be able to find it.

00:45:55.200 --> 00:45:58.230
I guess, I can't even open
this because it's frozen shut.

00:45:58.230 --> 00:45:59.063
Very good.

00:46:00.840 --> 00:46:02.960
And that's how you store that stuff.

00:46:02.960 --> 00:46:06.340
Like this is not Chernobyl-level radiation

00:46:06.340 --> 00:46:09.893
but it has still has to be
stored in a very special way.

00:46:11.770 --> 00:46:13.170
So there's that.

00:46:14.180 --> 00:46:17.420
We have to dispose of
radioactive materials

00:46:17.420 --> 00:46:18.270
in a certain way.

00:46:19.370 --> 00:46:22.297
So it goes into special containers

00:46:23.520 --> 00:46:26.433
and it just lives there
until somebody picks them up.

00:46:27.400 --> 00:46:28.773
These are centrifuges.

00:46:29.770 --> 00:46:31.870
If you want to separate different proteins

00:46:31.870 --> 00:46:34.010
or different parts of the cell,

00:46:34.010 --> 00:46:36.120
you can spin them at...

00:46:36.120 --> 00:46:38.850
Like this is the speed. So
that's rounds per minute.

00:46:38.850 --> 00:46:40.950
That's how quickly the centrifuge will go.

00:46:43.319 --> 00:46:46.923
You need a pretty strong motor and vacuum

00:46:46.923 --> 00:46:48.680
and all this stuff.

00:46:48.680 --> 00:46:50.760
And then there's a
bunch of other random...

00:46:50.760 --> 00:46:51.929
Oh yeah, a washing machine.

00:46:51.929 --> 00:46:54.003
So here's the funny thing about this.

00:46:55.310 --> 00:46:59.180
Because when it works, this thing...

00:46:59.180 --> 00:47:00.503
Like the rotor is spinning, obviously,

00:47:00.503 --> 00:47:02.700
at a super high speed.

00:47:02.700 --> 00:47:04.550
One thing you should never do

00:47:04.550 --> 00:47:07.023
when the centrifuge is
spinning, is move it.

00:47:09.080 --> 00:47:11.050
And it happens.

00:47:11.050 --> 00:47:11.900
And actually...

00:47:11.900 --> 00:47:13.669
I'm not sure if I...

00:47:13.669 --> 00:47:15.313
I need to turn it on to open it.

00:47:17.950 --> 00:47:22.400
If you look inside the rotor,
inside the chamber here,

00:47:22.400 --> 00:47:25.853
there's this big bump in the chamber.

00:47:26.750 --> 00:47:29.220
And that's because somebody
moved the centrifuge

00:47:30.580 --> 00:47:34.020
when it was on, and the rotor just ran off

00:47:34.020 --> 00:47:38.307
the axles it was spinning on,
and slammed into the wall.

00:47:38.307 --> 00:47:41.400
It was a big disaster.

00:47:41.400 --> 00:47:43.750
Well, disasters happen
in the lab all the time.

00:47:44.640 --> 00:47:47.840
So these are machines,
again, to separate proteins.

00:47:47.840 --> 00:47:51.233
This is called HPLC. It's
liquid chromatography.

00:47:52.670 --> 00:47:54.578
You can separate more
than proteins using these,

00:47:54.578 --> 00:47:56.170
but we use them for proteins.

00:47:56.170 --> 00:47:59.160
So what happens, it's the
same principle as the gel

00:47:59.160 --> 00:48:01.538
that we used for the Western blots.

00:48:01.538 --> 00:48:04.333
But in this case, we inject
proteins into this machine,

00:48:05.170 --> 00:48:07.750
and then they run through
of this little device.

00:48:07.750 --> 00:48:11.660
It's a column with media.

00:48:11.660 --> 00:48:15.683
And different proteins get
stuck at different times.

00:48:16.610 --> 00:48:18.470
And so you start with a mix of proteins

00:48:18.470 --> 00:48:21.500
but as you go, they go through the column.

00:48:21.500 --> 00:48:23.580
For example, small proteins will go first,

00:48:23.580 --> 00:48:25.890
large proteins will go last.

00:48:25.890 --> 00:48:28.280
And then you can harvest these proteins

00:48:28.280 --> 00:48:31.323
into these little tubes,
at different fractions,

00:48:32.330 --> 00:48:34.330
and then you can isolate these fractions,

00:48:34.330 --> 00:48:37.220
and do all kinds of science with them.

00:48:37.220 --> 00:48:38.610
If you need to break...

00:48:38.610 --> 00:48:39.443
Oh, yeah.

00:48:42.160 --> 00:48:44.190
There we go. That's the view.

00:48:44.190 --> 00:48:45.890
(Alexey laughs)

00:48:45.890 --> 00:48:47.700
We don't get to look out
over the window very much,

00:48:47.700 --> 00:48:48.773
but this is our view.

00:48:51.910 --> 00:48:53.243
That's the view.

00:48:54.803 --> 00:48:58.060
And that's the cold room.
That's where we store stuff.

00:48:58.060 --> 00:48:59.780
That's where we do some experiments, too.

00:48:59.780 --> 00:49:03.083
It's basically a big room
that's set to four degrees.

00:49:03.970 --> 00:49:07.810
We have another one that
used to be set to 37 degrees,

00:49:07.810 --> 00:49:10.810
but right now we just basically
turned into a storage room,

00:49:10.810 --> 00:49:13.420
because we don't work with
these little ciliates anymore

00:49:13.420 --> 00:49:15.800
that I told you about a while ago.

00:49:15.800 --> 00:49:18.633
So I'm gonna switch to my own face.

00:49:21.570 --> 00:49:22.403
No. Hold on.

00:49:24.009 --> 00:49:25.420
There we go.

00:49:25.420 --> 00:49:27.010
And I'm gonna return back to the office

00:49:27.010 --> 00:49:28.690
because it is already 11 o'clock.

00:49:28.690 --> 00:49:31.920
And I think you guys have
another class in about a minute,

00:49:31.920 --> 00:49:34.120
or I don't know how
school works these days.

00:49:38.210 --> 00:49:43.137
Yes. While I'm walking, come
up with a couple of questions.

00:49:53.490 --> 00:49:54.907
Very educational.

00:49:57.660 --> 00:50:00.597
Okay. I will turn this off.

00:50:09.616 --> 00:50:10.890
If you guys like looking at the equipment,

00:50:10.890 --> 00:50:14.214
wait until the pandemic
is over and come back,

00:50:14.214 --> 00:50:16.560
come to actual labs.

00:50:16.560 --> 00:50:18.450
The thing is thinking about lab equipment,

00:50:18.450 --> 00:50:20.660
all equipment is the same in every lab.

00:50:20.660 --> 00:50:22.230
You don't have to come to Rockefeller

00:50:22.230 --> 00:50:23.430
to look at a centrifuge.

00:50:27.040 --> 00:50:28.410
- I think Nora had a question

00:50:28.410 --> 00:50:30.783
about how did you get into the field?

00:50:32.000 --> 00:50:34.623
- Yeah, so I grew up in Russia.

00:50:36.010 --> 00:50:38.770
I went to school there.

00:50:38.770 --> 00:50:42.820
And then basically the choice was,

00:50:42.820 --> 00:50:47.663
do I go to biology in
college or do I do medicine?

00:50:48.740 --> 00:50:50.630
And then Russia's system
was completely different.

00:50:50.630 --> 00:50:53.530
It's not like med school is
super prestigious or anything.

00:50:54.480 --> 00:50:57.370
But, still, biology teachers make nothing

00:50:57.370 --> 00:51:00.243
and doctors can can
make a little bit more.

00:51:01.370 --> 00:51:03.290
And so I went to med school,

00:51:03.290 --> 00:51:06.560
while not really wanting
to do medicine on.

00:51:06.560 --> 00:51:09.940
And then when I was probably
my fourth year there

00:51:09.940 --> 00:51:14.390
in med school, in Russia, I
basically got an internship

00:51:14.390 --> 00:51:17.650
in a research lab in US.

00:51:17.650 --> 00:51:22.493
And I came to the US for like
a little four month stint.

00:51:23.410 --> 00:51:26.600
I almost failed my medical exams
because of that, in Russia,

00:51:26.600 --> 00:51:30.290
because I missed like
four months out of a year.

00:51:30.290 --> 00:51:33.220
And I loved it. I was super excited.

00:51:33.220 --> 00:51:35.970
Actually, I did the
protein crystallography.

00:51:35.970 --> 00:51:37.150
I was very happy to do that.

00:51:37.150 --> 00:51:40.890
And I came back for grad
school two years later.

00:51:40.890 --> 00:51:42.290
And I worked on fruit flies.

00:51:43.230 --> 00:51:45.550
Studied fly o-genuses

00:51:45.550 --> 00:51:49.310
And how the o-site is specified,

00:51:49.310 --> 00:51:52.783
and how female fertility
sterility works in fruit flies.

00:51:53.840 --> 00:51:57.630
And then after that, I realized that,

00:51:57.630 --> 00:51:59.350
stuff that happens in the nucleus

00:51:59.350 --> 00:52:01.510
is probably the most important part,

00:52:01.510 --> 00:52:03.360
so I want to study that.

00:52:03.360 --> 00:52:04.850
And if you want to study
stuff in the nucleus,

00:52:04.850 --> 00:52:07.723
you really want to start with this.

00:52:10.660 --> 00:52:12.033
And then I came here.

00:52:13.540 --> 00:52:15.230
I think, as in colleges too,

00:52:15.230 --> 00:52:16.790
you apply to multiple, different places.

00:52:16.790 --> 00:52:18.300
You interview in
multiple, different places

00:52:18.300 --> 00:52:20.700
and you basically see
what's the better fit.

00:52:20.700 --> 00:52:22.380
I thought I would work on small RNAs.

00:52:22.380 --> 00:52:27.380
When I was in college, microRNAs
and siRNAs were super hot.

00:52:27.390 --> 00:52:29.240
Everybody loved small RNAs.

00:52:29.240 --> 00:52:31.845
And I rotated in the
lab that studied them,

00:52:31.845 --> 00:52:33.497
and I was like, "No, this is boring."

00:52:39.360 --> 00:52:41.507
What I find boring, I'm sure
some people will be like,

00:52:41.507 --> 00:52:43.270
"Oh my god, this is the most
exciting thing in the world."

00:52:43.270 --> 00:52:47.593
So I'm not judging. I'm just
like, not for me at all.

00:52:53.460 --> 00:52:54.543
Anything else?

00:52:55.760 --> 00:52:58.680
Protein structure is important.
Enzymes are important.

00:52:58.680 --> 00:53:00.680
Substrates are important.

00:53:00.680 --> 00:53:02.020
Amino acids.

00:53:02.020 --> 00:53:02.853
If you know that the difference

00:53:02.853 --> 00:53:06.430
between lysine and glutamic acid,

00:53:06.430 --> 00:53:09.113
you know more than most people.

00:53:09.950 --> 00:53:14.950
So you got to know K's, R's,
E's, and D's, and that's all.

00:53:16.810 --> 00:53:19.340
And also, you know, the
glycine breaks the alpha helix.

00:53:19.340 --> 00:53:20.840
That's also cool.

00:53:20.840 --> 00:53:22.160
If you put a glycine into the protein,

00:53:22.160 --> 00:53:23.483
it will break the helix.

00:53:29.220 --> 00:53:31.360
Yes. Centrifuges.

00:53:31.360 --> 00:53:33.590
So when I worked in Russia,

00:53:33.590 --> 00:53:35.730
we didn't have these nice centrifuges.

00:53:35.730 --> 00:53:39.040
We had a plastic centrifuge
that it was really terrible.

00:53:39.040 --> 00:53:41.250
But we figured out a
way how to isolate DNA

00:53:41.250 --> 00:53:44.220
on the centrifuge, and we did that.

00:53:44.220 --> 00:53:49.220
And I don't wish that upon
anyone. But we did that.

00:53:53.980 --> 00:53:55.450
We washed the pipette tips,

00:53:55.450 --> 00:53:56.638
and we washed the tubes, too.

00:53:56.638 --> 00:53:57.540
That was hilarious.

00:53:57.540 --> 00:54:01.210
I worked in a research
institute that the project was,

00:54:01.210 --> 00:54:05.770
we studied tuberculosis
in HIV positive patients.

00:54:05.770 --> 00:54:09.180
And we washed the tips and plastic

00:54:09.180 --> 00:54:11.590
from our bio material.

00:54:11.590 --> 00:54:15.670
Here, it would be like a
biosafety level three, probably.

00:54:15.670 --> 00:54:18.500
You would need to wear a full
hazmat suit to work with that.

00:54:18.500 --> 00:54:23.500
There it's like, "Ah, reuse it."

00:54:23.552 --> 00:54:26.219
(Alexey laughs)

00:54:30.610 --> 00:54:31.763
Wow.

00:54:32.810 --> 00:54:36.603
That is not easy.

00:54:39.030 --> 00:54:39.983
Wow.

00:54:42.250 --> 00:54:45.920
So you sequenced it or how...

00:54:45.920 --> 00:54:47.170
How did you figure it out?

00:54:47.170 --> 00:54:48.063
Wow.

00:54:51.110 --> 00:54:54.130
So there's a lot of interesting
math with DNA, right?

00:54:54.130 --> 00:54:55.340
DNA is only four base pairs

00:54:55.340 --> 00:54:57.950
and it's the same base pairs
all across the animal kingdom.

00:54:57.950 --> 00:55:00.040
There is one interesting
hint you can get very quickly

00:55:00.040 --> 00:55:04.687
from DNA because some
organisms methylate their DNA

00:55:04.687 --> 00:55:06.350
and some don't.

00:55:06.350 --> 00:55:08.050
For example, if you work with humans,

00:55:08.050 --> 00:55:11.020
like 10% of our cytosines are methylated.

00:55:11.020 --> 00:55:13.313
They have methylcytosine instead.

00:55:14.690 --> 00:55:17.670
Oh yeah. You can do specific
PCR primers, of course.

00:55:17.670 --> 00:55:20.990
But for example, if you
work with fruit flies,

00:55:20.990 --> 00:55:23.340
they don't have DNA methylation.

00:55:23.340 --> 00:55:24.770
And so it's really funny
when people are trying

00:55:24.770 --> 00:55:26.100
to use fruit flies as a model

00:55:26.100 --> 00:55:28.110
for a lot of epigenetic processes.

00:55:28.110 --> 00:55:30.770
And I love fruit flies, I worked
with them for a long time.

00:55:30.770 --> 00:55:34.560
But you cannot always study
human stuff in fruit flies,

00:55:34.560 --> 00:55:37.610
just because they don't have
the same regulatory systems.

00:55:37.610 --> 00:55:38.860
But obviously if you realize

00:55:38.860 --> 00:55:41.170
that you don't have DNA
methylation in your sample,

00:55:41.170 --> 00:55:42.770
you know that this is not human DNA,

00:55:42.770 --> 00:55:46.290
this is like fruit fly DNA
or something like that.

00:55:46.290 --> 00:55:48.780
Another interesting question is,

00:55:48.780 --> 00:55:53.070
if human genome is 3
billion base pairs long,

00:55:53.070 --> 00:55:57.120
how long should the
primer be to be specific?

00:55:57.120 --> 00:55:58.720
And you have two primers, right?

00:55:59.750 --> 00:56:02.100
So you can do the math.

00:56:02.100 --> 00:56:04.553
I can give you the answer, whatever.

00:56:05.850 --> 00:56:07.020
It's actually quite interesting

00:56:07.020 --> 00:56:11.210
because it's an important consideration.

00:56:11.210 --> 00:56:14.480
Because when you do a
PCR from bacterial DNA

00:56:15.380 --> 00:56:16.920
or especially bacterial plasmid,

00:56:16.920 --> 00:56:20.533
which is 10,000 base pairs,

00:56:21.900 --> 00:56:26.010
even a six base fragment
only really happens once.

00:56:26.010 --> 00:56:28.063
When you do a PCR from a human DNA,

00:56:29.900 --> 00:56:32.260
you really have to make sure
your primers are specific.

00:56:32.260 --> 00:56:33.660
And that they're long enough

00:56:33.660 --> 00:56:36.330
that they only happen only a
couple of times in the genome.

00:56:36.330 --> 00:56:38.780
Because let's say they happen twice,

00:56:38.780 --> 00:56:41.090
but you only get a product
if your primers are

00:56:41.090 --> 00:56:44.313
in the same locus, if
they run into each other.

00:56:47.370 --> 00:56:51.323
Yes. So the jelly line
substance, that's agarose gel.

00:56:52.800 --> 00:56:55.480
Actually the guy who invented
this, I met him in person.

00:56:55.480 --> 00:56:58.703
He was super old by then.
His name was Oliver Smithies.

00:56:59.780 --> 00:57:03.600
He was like really funny, old dude.

00:57:03.600 --> 00:57:08.600
He came up with the idea to
resolve DNA fragments in starch.

00:57:08.860 --> 00:57:12.450
So he had like potato
starch or corn starch.

00:57:12.450 --> 00:57:14.900
I think it was potato starch back then.

00:57:14.900 --> 00:57:16.210
And he made this jelly,

00:57:16.210 --> 00:57:20.280
and he put DNA in there
and applied current.

00:57:20.280 --> 00:57:22.640
And you could see that
small fragments run faster

00:57:22.640 --> 00:57:24.000
than big ones.

00:57:24.000 --> 00:57:25.260
Again, because big ones...

00:57:25.260 --> 00:57:26.093
Yup. Yeah.

00:57:26.093 --> 00:57:27.760
So big ones get stuck,

00:57:27.760 --> 00:57:30.623
and small ones just wiggled right through.

00:57:33.080 --> 00:57:35.910
And then, as technology progressed,

00:57:35.910 --> 00:57:39.110
they replaced the starch
gel, which is super fragile.

00:57:39.110 --> 00:57:40.870
It breaks apart very quickly.

00:57:40.870 --> 00:57:42.980
They replaced the starch
gel with agarose gel.

00:57:42.980 --> 00:57:45.620
And agarose is obviously
much easier to work with.

00:57:45.620 --> 00:57:48.883
You can make nice stuff
in shapes out of it.

00:57:51.200 --> 00:57:53.260
Yeah, no. You guys are lucky.
You've seen all these things.

00:57:53.260 --> 00:57:57.190
I first saw that when I was in
not so much even in college.

00:57:57.190 --> 00:57:58.777
I came to the lab and I'm like,

00:57:58.777 --> 00:58:00.850
"Oh my god, this is the ethidium DNA.

00:58:00.850 --> 00:58:04.160
This is magic, it fluoresces. Super cool."

00:58:04.160 --> 00:58:06.060
And then, of course, you don't
know all the precautions,

00:58:06.060 --> 00:58:10.380
so you start looking at it
without the face shield.

00:58:10.380 --> 00:58:13.420
And then you think, "Why
are my eyes itching?"

00:58:13.420 --> 00:58:15.246
I know why they're itching.

00:58:15.246 --> 00:58:19.643
Because I was looking at
the gels for 30 minutes.

00:58:25.240 --> 00:58:28.115
When do you guys have to go?
I can keep talking forever.

00:58:28.115 --> 00:58:30.167
Just let me know. Cut me off.

00:58:37.590 --> 00:58:40.463
I don't hear anything. Is anybody talking?

00:58:42.400 --> 00:58:45.240
- [Lecturer] Yeah. I mean,
technically our class is over.

00:58:45.240 --> 00:58:48.730
Does anybody have any other
questions to finish up?

00:58:48.730 --> 00:58:50.636
We have a double period,
so they don't have to.

00:58:50.636 --> 00:58:52.680
- Ah, I see. Ah, okay, cool.

00:58:52.680 --> 00:58:53.570
- [Lecturer] Yeah, we do like...

00:58:53.570 --> 00:58:55.120
The first half is together,

00:58:55.120 --> 00:58:56.920
and the second half is asynchronous.

00:58:57.834 --> 00:58:59.880
- So where do you want to
be when you're finished?

00:58:59.880 --> 00:59:01.460
There's a question in chat.

00:59:01.460 --> 00:59:02.883
That is a great question.

00:59:04.080 --> 00:59:05.350
To be honest, I don't know.

00:59:05.350 --> 00:59:06.970
I'm applying for jobs right now.

00:59:06.970 --> 00:59:08.430
And when I say I'm applying for jobs,

00:59:08.430 --> 00:59:12.690
I'm applying for jobs in
academia as a lab head,

00:59:12.690 --> 00:59:15.790
as a person who will run his own lab.

00:59:15.790 --> 00:59:18.270
I just had an interview in England.

00:59:18.270 --> 00:59:23.110
I just scheduled an interview in NIH,

00:59:23.110 --> 00:59:24.610
National Institutes of Health.

00:59:26.650 --> 00:59:27.490
We'll see how it goes.

00:59:27.490 --> 00:59:29.550
This year has been really weird

00:59:29.550 --> 00:59:33.023
because teaching universities
are essentially not hiring.

00:59:34.100 --> 00:59:37.590
So the amount of jobs that
are out there are pretty...

00:59:39.010 --> 00:59:40.790
But on the other hand, a lot of people

00:59:40.790 --> 00:59:42.320
are not going on the job market this year.

00:59:42.320 --> 00:59:45.453
So just might as well try it out.

00:59:46.780 --> 00:59:48.800
I have a wife and I have two kids.

00:59:48.800 --> 00:59:52.260
My wife is also a scientist.
She's a post-doc at NYU.

00:59:52.260 --> 00:59:54.483
Just 30 blocks that way.

00:59:56.200 --> 00:59:58.280
We have to coordinate
how we apply for jobs,

00:59:58.280 --> 00:59:59.805
because if I get the job in England

00:59:59.805 --> 01:00:02.420
and she stays in New York with...

01:00:05.690 --> 01:00:08.210
Can we leave? Yeah, you
can feel free to leave.

01:00:08.210 --> 01:00:11.190
I'm not clocking anybody's hours.

01:00:11.190 --> 01:00:14.340
Yeah. So it's an interesting
stuff with academia.

01:00:14.340 --> 01:00:17.900
I think the draw of academia

01:00:17.900 --> 01:00:20.040
is that you're sort of your own boss.

01:00:20.040 --> 01:00:22.080
You do your own things,

01:00:22.080 --> 01:00:25.693
and you kind of make your own schedule.

01:00:26.840 --> 01:00:31.840
The downside is that you
spend 25 plus years in school.

01:00:32.220 --> 01:00:34.860
You're super overqualified.

01:00:34.860 --> 01:00:37.800
And you know this one little thing better

01:00:37.800 --> 01:00:39.350
than anybody else in the world,

01:00:40.250 --> 01:00:42.990
but does anybody care?

01:00:42.990 --> 01:00:44.540
That's another question, right?

01:00:46.552 --> 01:00:48.670
And then do you make an impact too?

01:00:48.670 --> 01:00:49.900
That's all something you ask yourself.

01:00:49.900 --> 01:00:51.620
Like if you're studying
fruit fly o-genuses

01:00:51.620 --> 01:00:54.600
for the rest of your life, does it matter?

01:00:54.600 --> 01:00:56.200
And sometimes it does.

01:00:56.200 --> 01:01:01.200
The guys who studied the
bacterial immunity systems, right?

01:01:01.460 --> 01:01:02.870
They were like, "Oh, this is so cool.

01:01:02.870 --> 01:01:06.410
We have this bacteria with
strange looking DNA sequences

01:01:06.410 --> 01:01:08.780
that repeat themselves.

01:01:08.780 --> 01:01:13.700
Let's call them clustered
repeats interspersed elements,

01:01:13.700 --> 01:01:16.490
blah, blah, blah, something cool."

01:01:16.490 --> 01:01:18.817
And then 10 years later,
somebody else was like,

01:01:18.817 --> 01:01:22.590
"Wait a second. We can repurpose
that and edit human genome.

01:01:22.590 --> 01:01:24.680
or any genome like this."

01:01:24.680 --> 01:01:27.200
And all of a sudden, that's
the hottest thing in the world.

01:01:27.200 --> 01:01:28.513
And this is super cool.

01:01:29.590 --> 01:01:30.990
Why are fruit flies used so much?

01:01:30.990 --> 01:01:32.650
That's a great question.

01:01:32.650 --> 01:01:35.053
They are historical.

01:01:36.320 --> 01:01:39.600
Louis the XIV, in his
court had fruit flies,

01:01:39.600 --> 01:01:41.570
and they were looking at their eye color,

01:01:41.570 --> 01:01:42.620
and that it was cool.

01:01:44.605 --> 01:01:47.300
And, of course, Thomas Hunt
Morgan, the guy from Caltech

01:01:47.300 --> 01:01:49.270
who used fruit flies.

01:01:49.270 --> 01:01:51.090
He was a very prolific scientist.

01:01:51.090 --> 01:01:54.320
And people he trained,
every single one of them

01:01:54.320 --> 01:01:59.080
went to be Nobel prize winning
or deserving scientists,

01:01:59.080 --> 01:02:01.070
who did fantastic job in fruit flies.

01:02:01.070 --> 01:02:04.570
So basically, the basis
of our animal genetics

01:02:04.570 --> 01:02:06.380
was laid out in fruit flies.

01:02:06.380 --> 01:02:09.880
Why it was fruit flies and not
worms? Just the sheer luck.

01:02:09.880 --> 01:02:13.000
He could have studied C elegans and worms.

01:02:13.000 --> 01:02:15.103
He would have done it
in some other organisms.

01:02:17.510 --> 01:02:19.220
It's a mix of history and just utility.

01:02:19.220 --> 01:02:20.760
Flies are very useful.

01:02:20.760 --> 01:02:24.160
The generation time is just 14 days.

01:02:24.160 --> 01:02:27.040
They live, literally, on garbage.

01:02:27.040 --> 01:02:32.040
Like the fly food is corn,
sugar, and yeast and water.

01:02:33.390 --> 01:02:35.810
And you mix that and you boil
it and you pour it into vials.

01:02:35.810 --> 01:02:37.720
And you can have that vial,
they live for 90 days,

01:02:37.720 --> 01:02:40.650
you can watch them for a long enough time.

01:02:40.650 --> 01:02:42.600
They propagate almost indefinitely.

01:02:42.600 --> 01:02:44.500
It's very hard to kill them, literally.

01:02:44.500 --> 01:02:46.890
You have to try very
hard to kill your flies.

01:02:46.890 --> 01:02:48.453
I've done that accidentally.

01:02:50.110 --> 01:02:53.133
So they're very useful models.

01:02:54.438 --> 01:02:56.140
And also inertia, right?

01:02:56.140 --> 01:02:58.950
By now, there is such a bank of mutants.

01:02:58.950 --> 01:03:00.100
They're so well-characterized

01:03:00.100 --> 01:03:03.627
that sometimes you want to
study something and you're like,

01:03:03.627 --> 01:03:05.660
"I wonder if there is
a fly mutant for that."

01:03:05.660 --> 01:03:07.000
And probably there is.

01:03:07.000 --> 01:03:09.830
And you just go in and you figure out

01:03:09.830 --> 01:03:11.250
what people already published on it.

01:03:11.250 --> 01:03:13.310
And sometimes you inform
your decisions based on

01:03:13.310 --> 01:03:14.872
on that mutant.

01:03:14.872 --> 01:03:16.570
We do more mouse work,

01:03:16.570 --> 01:03:19.980
because mice are a lot closer
to humans just in everything.

01:03:19.980 --> 01:03:22.900
They have DNA methylation
among other things.

01:03:22.900 --> 01:03:24.970
But now, with the next gen sequencing,

01:03:24.970 --> 01:03:27.260
and I'm not sure if that
point really came across,

01:03:27.260 --> 01:03:29.543
because I was talking very quickly.

01:03:31.520 --> 01:03:35.080
What we base a lot of our hypothesis on

01:03:35.080 --> 01:03:37.440
is just patient sequencing data.

01:03:37.440 --> 01:03:39.700
There is enough resources
out there that you can go in

01:03:39.700 --> 01:03:43.610
and just look up a specific type of cancer

01:03:43.610 --> 01:03:45.070
or your favorite protein,

01:03:45.070 --> 01:03:47.890
and you can find which
cancer it's mutated in.

01:03:47.890 --> 01:03:50.760
Or which cancers have a mutation

01:03:50.760 --> 01:03:52.873
in whatever else you want to study.

01:03:53.860 --> 01:03:55.713
And for example, histone mutations,

01:03:56.610 --> 01:03:58.240
something that happened
in the last 10 years,

01:03:58.240 --> 01:03:59.440
less than 10 years,

01:03:59.440 --> 01:04:02.340
is that they have
started finding mutations

01:04:02.340 --> 01:04:04.310
in these very tails.

01:04:04.310 --> 01:04:06.083
And very specific mutations.

01:04:08.180 --> 01:04:09.013
Histones are very funny

01:04:09.013 --> 01:04:12.200
because normally you
have one gene per protein

01:04:12.200 --> 01:04:13.800
and that's like dogma.

01:04:13.800 --> 01:04:14.880
So for most histones,

01:04:14.880 --> 01:04:19.070
there's about between 13 and
15 genes for each histone.

01:04:19.910 --> 01:04:22.470
So that makes histone
genetics very complicated

01:04:22.470 --> 01:04:25.680
because you can just make
a null of knockout, right?

01:04:25.680 --> 01:04:29.090
You have to make 15 knockouts,

01:04:29.090 --> 01:04:30.390
sometimes on the same chromosome,

01:04:30.390 --> 01:04:32.983
which just is impossible to do.

01:04:34.390 --> 01:04:36.610
There are ways around it,
but it's very, very hard.

01:04:36.610 --> 01:04:37.640
But what you can do now,

01:04:37.640 --> 01:04:40.610
is you can just look at patient data sets

01:04:40.610 --> 01:04:41.760
and that's your genetic.

01:04:41.760 --> 01:04:44.610
That's your work bench, essentially,

01:04:44.610 --> 01:04:48.040
instead of growing flies and
running these screens on flies.

01:04:48.040 --> 01:04:51.330
You look at thousands
of cancers and you find,

01:04:51.330 --> 01:04:54.500
Oh my god, this particular
residue is mutated

01:04:54.500 --> 01:04:59.040
in like 90% of patients
with glioma for example.

01:04:59.040 --> 01:05:00.050
And that's true.

01:05:00.050 --> 01:05:04.633
This K27, lysine 27, on H3 tail.

01:05:05.620 --> 01:05:06.610
I'm pointing at it.

01:05:06.610 --> 01:05:09.860
I can't even find it right
now. I think it's old.

01:05:09.860 --> 01:05:12.690
You can't even see it, but it's
there. It's on these tails.

01:05:12.690 --> 01:05:16.120
It is mutated specifically to methionine

01:05:17.320 --> 01:05:19.530
in patients with these gliomas.

01:05:19.530 --> 01:05:21.090
And these are pediatric patients

01:05:21.090 --> 01:05:23.850
which, invariably, it's a lethal cancer.

01:05:23.850 --> 01:05:24.903
You can't cure it.

01:05:26.400 --> 01:05:31.040
And since we found that
mutation in the last five years,

01:05:31.040 --> 01:05:33.080
we've actually come very, very close

01:05:33.080 --> 01:05:35.680
to figuring out what we can do to fix it.

01:05:35.680 --> 01:05:38.200
So, you know, five more years,
we might be able to fix it.

01:05:38.200 --> 01:05:39.840
It's not gonna help people who have it now

01:05:39.840 --> 01:05:42.350
but it might help somebody
five, 10 years down the road.

01:05:42.350 --> 01:05:46.850
And it all started with
the Louis experiments

01:05:46.850 --> 01:05:51.133
with a fly with extra bristles on its leg.

01:05:54.910 --> 01:05:57.273
So that's how it works.
And science is fun.

01:06:00.300 --> 01:06:02.680
- [Lecturer] Well, I think
that's a great note to end on,

01:06:02.680 --> 01:06:04.630
potentially, that science is fun.

01:06:04.630 --> 01:06:06.760
And there are really cool things

01:06:06.760 --> 01:06:09.100
that will come from this research

01:06:09.100 --> 01:06:10.400
over the next several years.

01:06:10.400 --> 01:06:12.500
So thank you so, so
much for talking to us,

01:06:12.500 --> 01:06:13.940
and showing us around your lab.

01:06:13.940 --> 01:06:15.293
We really appreciate it.

01:06:16.360 --> 01:06:18.660
And yeah, thanks again then, students.

01:06:18.660 --> 01:06:20.670
I will see you tomorrow.

01:06:20.670 --> 01:06:23.390
- Thanks for being around.
And thanks for listening.

01:06:23.390 --> 01:06:26.430
If you got like 1/10 of
what I rambled about,

01:06:26.430 --> 01:06:27.770
that'd be fantastic.

01:06:27.770 --> 01:06:29.083
So thank you.

